{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-7988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-7988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get]\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-7988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "616afab6d1b3ac2d84eca5bbf5be511c9e5b14d20892c108867c4a49e84dda0e"} {"question" "q-7988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-7988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{7}}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{7}}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-7988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-89345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/90b505954bb714ad86f60e07f7a26c16c954139689d96684ed553a7b494f0527.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-89345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/b43dcf6c343994e3226e2840a0ae80f60484c8de5d7984f61ec6447185181a76.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ and $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the right branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the right branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-89345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "616afab6d1b3ac2d84eca5bbf5be511c9e5b14d20892c108867c4a49e84dda0e"} {"question" "q-89345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-89345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "t\\in (-\\pi ,0),\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "t\\in (0,\\pi ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-89345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/a1506b2272710ce21e0c58d327738dbe4afb3b134981a239e6e7882c3efb7072.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(4,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=4.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "616afab6d1b3ac2d84eca5bbf5be511c9e5b14d20892c108867c4a49e84dda0e"} {"question" "q-325917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sec(t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "616afab6d1b3ac2d84eca5bbf5be511c9e5b14d20892c108867c4a49e84dda0e"} {"question" "q-325940" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325940" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x^2}{81}-\\frac{y^2}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/7facf793b5ce6740a31d665d96b03dd591e7edc2a6c35e519fa3b805534e02c1.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$\n\n$t\\in [$\n$-\\pi ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/80e49fe1187d1b9e42ef6e221568c9bafca1507238cc644afbda695f5cee2539.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ }\\cup \\text{ }(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "616afab6d1b3ac2d84eca5bbf5be511c9e5b14d20892c108867c4a49e84dda0e"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"} {"question" "q-325981" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d88dc7a154d08b23bc4da022eb0676601c08e461db08efbe96468b72ec61cb2"}] "usage" {"q-89345" []} "basis" 2109 "retractions" [[17592186088392 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/q-89345.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\sqrt{2}\\sec t, y = 2\\tan t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186087283 17592186087284 17592186087285 17592186087286 17592186087287] "immutable_fields" [[17592186087282 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087282 :answer-field/id] [17592186087282 :answer-field/key] [17592186087282 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
